Cloud Concepts Reflection

Before learning about cloud computing,
I did not realize that I was already using 
cloud services almost every day.Some of the 
applications I use regularly are Google Drive, 
Facebook Messenger, and Canva. Google Drive 
helps me store important files and access them 
from different devices, which is very useful for 
school. Messenger allows me to communicate with 
my classmates, friends, and family anytime.
Canva is one of my favorite tools because it 
helps me create presentations, posters, and other 
school requirements quickly and easily.

These three applications are examples of Software 
as a Service (SaaS) because they provide 
ready-to-use software through the internet. I do 
not need to install or manage the systems behind 
these applications. They also use the Public Cloud 
deployment model because anyone can create an 
account and use their services online. The companies 
that provide these services handle the maintenance, 
updates, and security, while users simply use the 
features they offer.

I also learned why version control is important, 
especially in cloud-based projects. When many people 
work on the same project, changes can happen at the 
same time. Without version control, files can be lost 
or overwritten, making teamwork difficult. Git helps 
record every change, making it easier to return to an 
earlier version if a mistake happens.

GitHub makes collaboration much easier because it 
allows team members to share their work, review changes, 
and combine updates without affecting the main project immediately. 
It also keeps a history of every change, making it easy to see who 
made specific updates. Overall, this lesson helped me understand 
that cloud computing is already a part of my daily life, and tools 
like Git and GitHub make teamwork more organized, efficient, and 
reliable.
